From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] Modeling Algorithms - BRepGProp_Gauss keeps the by-plane mass
 instead of discarding it

BRepGProp_Gauss::convert, the six-argument overload, computes the mass and then
throws it away for every by-plane computation:

  convert(theInertia, theOutGravityCenter, theOutMatrixOfInertia, theOutMass);
  if (std::abs(theInertia.Mass) >= EPS_DIM && theIsByPoint)
  {
    const double anInvMass = 1.0 / theInertia.Mass;
    if (theIsByPoint)  { ... theCoeff[i] + theInertia.I* * anInvMass ... }
    else               { ... theInertia.I* * anInvMass ... }
    theOutMass = theInertia.Mass;
  }
  else
  {
    theOutMass = 0.0;
    theOutGravityCenter.SetCoord(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
  }

The four-argument convert on the first line has already set theOutMass
correctly. The && theIsByPoint in the condition below it then sends every
by-plane call into the else, which was written for the vanishing-mass case, and
that else overwrites the mass with 0.0 and the gravity centre with (0, 0, 0).
The inner if (theIsByPoint) ... else ... is dead in its else branch for the same
reason, and its presence is the evidence the outer condition was meant to test
EPS_DIM alone: there is no other way for that branch to be reached.

All four by-plane BRepGProp_Vinert::Perform overloads are affected, and so are
the four gp_Pln constructors that delegate to them. They are the only callers
that pass theIsByPoint == false (BRepGProp_Vinert.cxx:285, :300, :317, plus the
:263 overload that forwards to :273), and both BRepGProp_Gauss::Compute paths,
the adaptive one at BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx:1101 and the plain one at :1388, reach
this convert. BRepGProp_Sinert and BRepGProp_Cinert are unaffected: Compute
calls the six-argument convert only when myType == Vinert and otherwise calls
the four-argument one, which has no such flag.

Dropping && theIsByPoint is the whole fix, and it makes the inner else live and
correct rather than merely reachable. For a by-point computation theCoeff is a
three-element translation, theOrigin - loc (BRepGProp_Vinert.cxx:238), so the
gravity centre is theCoeff[i] + I*/mass. For a by-plane computation theCoeff is
the four plane coefficients a, b, c, d, with d re-based on loc
(BRepGProp_Vinert.cxx:279), which is not a translation and must not be added to
anything; the correct by-plane gravity centre is I*/mass relative to loc, which
is exactly what the inner else computes and what the four-argument convert on
the first line had already written. The matrix of inertia is assigned
unconditionally after the branch and does not change.

Measured on macOS arm64 against the pinned V8_0_1 asset by
Scripts/repro/2827/probe.mm, per face with the BRepGProp_Domain loaded so the
integral is over the trimmed region, alongside
BRepGProp::VolumePropertiesGK(S, Props, thePln, 1e-6, true, true, false) over
the same shape as the independent construction:

  fixture and plane                   Vinert by plane   VolumePropertiesGK
  10-cube, plane z = 0                              0   999.9999999999999
  10-cube, plane z = -100                           0   1000.000000000002
  10-cube, oblique plane                            0   1000
  20x20x2 plate, radius-3 hole, z = 0               0   743.4513322353838
  cylinder r = 5 h = 10, plane z = 0                0   785.3981633974456

The by-point Vinert sum over the same faces is 999.9999999999998,
743.4513322353837 and 785.3981633974482, and BRepGProp::VolumeProperties reports
999.9999999999998, 743.4513322353836 and 785.3981633974482 for the three shapes.
So the by-plane number is not the integrand vanishing, and not a plane placed
where the answer happens to be zero: the same three shapes measured through
OCCT's own by-plane entry point give the volume to the last few digits.

That entry point is why the kernel does not meet the defect itself. It goes
through BRepGProp_VinertGK and math_KronrodSingleIntegration, a separate
integrator that never reaches this function, and BRepGProp.cxx:311 is OCCT's only
BRepGProp_Vinert call site and passes a point.

Rebased onto upstream IR (53706f78a8): IR's BRepGPropTest.AdaptivePlaneLimitedInertia (#1640) pins the
matrix of inertia of a by-plane Vinert about the LOCATION (160, 104, 200, -72, -36, -48 for the box
[0, 3] x [0, 4] x [0, 2] against the plane z = 0), and does not check the mass. That is the effect of
this defect, not a property worth keeping: GProp_GProps::MatrixOfInertia() reduces the stored matrix
to the centre of mass with the stored mass and centre (GProp::HOperator), and the mass and centre
being zero made the reduction vanish. With the mass kept the matrix is the centroidal one, 40, 26
and 50 on the diagonal (m (b^2 + c^2) / 12 with m = 24) and zero off it, so the test now asserts the
mass, the centre (1.5, 2, 1) and those values.

SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift#2827.
---
 .../TKTopAlgo/BRepGProp/BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx          |  3 ++-
 .../TKTopAlgo/GTests/BRepGProp_Test.cxx              | 20 ++++++++++++++------
 2 files changed, 16 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/BRepGProp/BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/BRepGProp/BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx
index b0beff12f0..349e8a9b2b 100644
--- a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/BRepGProp/BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx
+++ b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/BRepGProp/BRepGProp_Gauss.cxx
@@ -451,7 +451,8 @@ void BRepGProp_Gauss::convert(const BRepGProp_Gauss::Inertia& theInertia,
                               double&                         theOutMass)
 {
   convert(theInertia, theOutGravityCenter, theOutMatrixOfInertia, theOutMass);
-  if (std::abs(theInertia.Mass) >= EPS_DIM && theIsByPoint)
+  // The by-plane case (theIsByPoint false) has a mass too, and the inner else branch is its centre.
+  if (std::abs(theInertia.Mass) >= EPS_DIM)
   {
     const double anInvMass = 1.0 / theInertia.Mass;
     if (theIsByPoint)
diff --git a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/GTests/BRepGProp_Test.cxx b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/GTests/BRepGProp_Test.cxx
index bc4f8a967a..267aedda70 100644
--- a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/GTests/BRepGProp_Test.cxx
+++ b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKTopAlgo/GTests/BRepGProp_Test.cxx
@@ -382,13 +382,21 @@ TEST(BRepGPropTest, AdaptivePlaneLimitedInertia)
   BRepGProp_Domain anAdaptiveDomain(aFace);
   BRepGProp_Vinert anAdaptive(anAdaptiveFace, anAdaptiveDomain, aReferencePlane, aLocation, 1.0e-8);
 
+  // The solid between the face and the plane is the 3 x 4 x 2 box [0, 3] x [0, 4] x [0, 2]: mass 24,
+  // centre (1.5, 2, 1). MatrixOfInertia is about the centre of mass, so it is m (b^2 + c^2) / 12 on
+  // the diagonal and zero off it. (The values about the location, 160, 104, 200, -72, -36 and -48,
+  // are what the matrix held while the by-plane mass and centre were discarded as zero.)
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptive.Mass(), 24.0, 1.0e-10);
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptive.CentreOfMass().X(), 1.5, 1.0e-10);
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptive.CentreOfMass().Y(), 2.0, 1.0e-10);
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptive.CentreOfMass().Z(), 1.0, 1.0e-10);
   const gp_Mat anAdaptiveInertia = anAdaptive.MatrixOfInertia();
-  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(1, 1), 160.0, 1.0e-10);
-  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(2, 2), 104.0, 1.0e-10);
-  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(3, 3), 200.0, 1.0e-10);
-  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(1, 2), -72.0, 1.0e-10);
-  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(1, 3), -36.0, 1.0e-10);
-  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(2, 3), -48.0, 1.0e-10);
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(1, 1), 40.0, 1.0e-10);
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(2, 2), 26.0, 1.0e-10);
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(3, 3), 50.0, 1.0e-10);
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(1, 2), 0.0, 1.0e-10);
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(1, 3), 0.0, 1.0e-10);
+  EXPECT_NEAR(anAdaptiveInertia.Value(2, 3), 0.0, 1.0e-10);
   expectGPropsNear(anAdaptive, aFixed, 1.0e-10);
 }
 
